   "source": [
    "# Introduction\n",
    "Cello Suite No. 1 by JS Bach is one of the most popular compositions for the cello. Unfortunately, our copy has been corrupted by a terrible noise! Listen to the WAV file \"sound_file_2022\" to hear for yourself. This Notebook will explore two different ways of filtering out this noise, namely a cascaded Resistor-Capacitor (RC) filter and a Butterworth filter. We will also be comparing the characteristics of the digital and analogue representations of each filter.\n",
    "\n",
    "First, let's import the song and analyse its spectrum. The module SciPy can do this for us (and a whole lot more) very easily."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "id": "7aadbec7-62fc-44aa-8856-d4c0d2b0561b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 700x600 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import scipy as sc\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from tabulate import tabulate\n",
    "\n",
    "fs, song = sc.io.wavfile.read(\"sound_file_2022.wav\")\n",
    "song = song.astype(np.float64)\n",
    "song /= np.max(np.abs(song)) # normalise\n",
    "L = len(song)\n",
    "Ts = 1/fs\n",
    "\n",
    "freq = sc.fft.rfftfreq(L, Ts)\n",
    "Y = (np.abs(sc.fft.rfft(song)))/L # fft calculation scales up by L, so we scale back down by L\n",
    "Y[1:-1] *= 2\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(7, 6))\n",
    "plt.plot(freq, Y)\n",
    "plt.title('Single-Sided FFT')\n",
    "plt.xlabel('Frequency (Hz)')\n",
    "plt.ylabel('Amplitude')\n",
    "caption = r\"$\\it{Figure~1}$: A single-sided FFT of the audio data.\"\n",
    "plt.figtext(0.5, 0.001, caption, wrap=True, horizontalalignment='center', fontsize=10);\n",
    "\n",
    "# from inspection, high frequency noise is around 3.5E03\n",
    "maxYBin = np.isclose(np.max(Y), Y).nonzero()[0][0]\n",
    "noisyFreq = freq[maxYBin]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2991cbde-934a-410c-958e-efa77a38f68d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "SciPy can read a WAV file and output its sampling frequency ($F_{S}$) and the audio data. Normalising this data ensures we don't blow out our eardrums accidentally after writing our filtered data to a WAV file. SciPy reads the audio in as integers, so we need to first convert it to a float type (since normalisation ensures all the numbers are between 0 and 1).\n",
    "\n",
    "Sound waves can be made from multiple sine waves added together. In the time domain, this can look very confusing and it's hard to extract useful information about the sound being heard. Fast Fourier Transforms (FFTs) can compute the frequencies and amplitudes of each sine wave, resulting in a graph that allows us to more easily isolate specific frequency bins.\n",
    "\n",
    "`rfftfreq` calculates the $x$-axis for us when supplied with the length of the data and the sampling period ($T_{S}$). `rfft` calculates the $y$-axis; it just needs the data as input. It's a bit out of scope to explain in detail how the FFT algorithm works, but SciPy has excellent documentation which I encourage you to peruse.\n",
    "\n",
    "If you're still confused about the functions used and the scaling of 2, then here is an explanation. Normally with FFT calculations (`fft` and `fftfreq`) on real data, the output is symmetric around the Nyquist frequency (which is half the sampling frequency). This makes half of the data redundant. We can avoid this by using these alternate functions designed for real input (as opposed to complex input).\n",
    "Finally, we multiply all elements except the first and last by 2. The reason for this is the aforementioned symmetry - the amplitudes are halved for each frequency bin that shares a negative frequency counterpart. 0Hz and the Nyquist frequency (the first and last bins) don't have negative counterparts. Technically speaking, odd-length signals don't have a Nyquist frequency bin and so should have their last element multiplied by 2. However, since it's such a high frequency and low amplitude (near the limit of human hearing and no power to it), then it's pointless to consider it, especially as our data is conveniently of even length :)\n",
    "\n",
    "Now it is time to inspect the plot itself. We can see three large peaks dominating the graph, the first starting at about 3.5E03Hz. We can safely assume that this is our noise that we want to get rid of. Since it is the largest peak, it is easy enough to get the actual value of $f_{n1}$ (the first frequency component of the noise wave).\n",
    "\n",
    "# Filtering\n",
    "Since the noise consists of high-frequency amplitudes, a lowpass filter can attenuate them and allow through low frequencies. We can choose a cutoff frequency $f_{c}$ where the attenuation will be -3dB. Not only do we have to consider what cutoff frequency to choose, but also the order. Higher orders create a response closer and closer to the ideal, i.e., the roll-off becomes steeper and steeper. This also introduces more complexity and ripples in the passband, so we'll aim to keep it simple with orders on the lower end for both types of filters. We'll go into some formulae that can help us decide what order to choose for both types of filter.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Cascaded RC\n",
    "### Digital Implementation\n",
    "As you hopefully already know, the RC circuit is probably the simplest filter to design, consisting of just a resistor and a capacitor connected in series. For a lowpass design, the output is taken from across the capacitor. Its associated transfer function is:\n",
    "$$H(s) = \\frac{1}{1+sRC}$$\n",
    "(Note: An n-th order filter can be represented by this formula raised to the power of n).\n",
    "\n",
    "The cutoff frequency (for a 1<sup>st</sup> order filter) is calculated as $f_{c} = \\frac{1}{2\\pi RC}$. I mentioned before that there will be a -3dB attenuation at the cutoff frequency, but with higher orders, the attenuation increases according to the formula below:\n",
    "$$20\\log_{10}{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)^n}$$\n",
    "$n$ is the order of the filter. So for $n=4$, we would arrive at an attenuation of approximately -12dB at our chosen cutoff frequency.\n",
    "You may now be thinking: \"won't the cutoff frequency calculation change for higher order filters? Also, how do we even make higher order filters?\" Well, I say be patient! I'll get to it in due time!\n",
    "\n",
    "But yes, you're right. Consider a 2<sup>nd</sup> order filter made with components $R_{1}, R_{2}, C_{1}, C_{2}$. Our cutoff frequency is now calculated as such:\n",
    "$$f_{c} = \\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{R_{1}C_{1}R_{2}C_{2}}}$$\n",
    "However! You can see that if you choose the same resistor and capacitor values, i.e., $R_{1}=R_{2}$ and $C_{1}=C_{2}$, then we resolve to the same formula as before.\n",
    "\n",
    "How do we pick a cutoff frequency for our filter? We can adopt a trial-and-error approach to pick $f_{c}$ and $n$, then plot the FFT of the resulting filtered data. It's fairly fast and simple. I dislike trial-and-error methods, especially for cases where there are more than one variable.\n",
    "\n",
    "Consider the magnitude response of an n-th order lowpass filter:\n",
    "$$\\left| G(j\\omega) \\right| = \\left(\\frac{1}{1+\\omega ^2 T^2}\\right)^n$$\n",
    "where $T = \\frac{1}{\\omega_{c}}$. After rearranging and scaling to Hz, we obtain:\n",
    "$$f_{c} = \\frac{f}{\\sqrt{G^{-\\frac{2}{n}}-1}}$$\n",
    "We can substitute $f_{n1}$ into $f$ and supply a range of orders to calculate a range of cutoff frequencies. I'll get to why in a minute, but I'll first address the unaccounted variable $G$.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Sound to Noise Ratio (SNR)\n",
    "The SNR is essentially an indicator of how strong the desired signal is compared to the noise. Measured in decibels, a higher value means better quality. The minimum recommended SNR I've found for audio is 60dB to 80dB, so let's define our target SNR somewhere in that range (I picked 70dB). To obtain our actual SNR, the signal power and noise power can be calculated by summing the squares of the amplitudes that correspond to the desired signal and the noise respectively. Then:\n",
    "$$SNR = 10\\log_{10}\\left(\\frac{P_{s}}{P_{n}}\\right)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "We can then use the formula $A_{filter} = -(SNR_{target} - SNR)$. The linear gain $G$ can be obtained by $10^{A_{filter}/20}$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Back to the reason why we calculate a range of cutoff frequencies: we don't want to have a low $f_{-3dB}$ since we'll attenuate too much of the song. We can plot both $f_{c}$ and $f_{-3dB}$ and pick a suitable order (see figure 2)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "b1a3f0b1-08a5-4233-bd00-5fbd241c5467",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
