   "id": "13f2bb88-6b9a-4443-b648-6b511f7e6a2b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "There we go! No more noisy peaks. Our new SNR matches the target we set earlier.\n",
    "\n",
    "You can ignore the details of the function `binomialCoeffs`; all it does it get the coefficients of a binomial expansion. Fun fact, the rows in Pascal's triangle correspond to the n-th power!\n",
    "We define the order of the filter, the cutoff frequency, and then calculate a capacitor value based on a standard resistor value. I picked 1k$\\ohm$. \n",
    "Scipy can calculate the digital filter from an analogue transfer function when also provided with the sampling frequency. You may have seen an alternative function (`filtfilt()`) which effectively doubles the filter order but introduces zero phase distortion. I've used `lfilt()` since I haven't been able to tell the difference while listening.\n",
    "\n",
    "Before we listen to the filtered song, let's design an analogue circuit analogous (get it?) to our digital filter.\n",
    "### Analogue Implementation\n",
    "How do we make an n-th order filter? Naively, we could simply daisy-chain the same RC circuit. Unfortunately, without a buffer, each stage loads the next, meaning the poles move. Typically, the transfer function for an n<sup>th</sup> order lowpass filter is just $(1+sRC)^{-n}$ (as mentioned above), but we need to have buffers in between each stage. A buffer is simply a non-inverting operational-amplifier (op-amp) with unity gain, so luckily there's not much added complexity. The circuit simulator I will be using to implement both analogue filters is MicroCap12, which is freely available online.\n",
    "<figure style=\"margin-left: 0; margin-right: auto;\">\n",
    "  <img src=\"cascaded_circuit.png\" style=\"width:100%; display:block; margin-left:0;\">\n",
    "  <figcaption style=\"margin-left:0;\">\n",
    "    <em>Figure 4.</em> The cascaded analogue circuit used in the analysis.\n",
    "  </figcaption>\n",
    "</figure>\n",
    "\n",
    "Using the same resistor and picking a standard capacitor value that's close to our calculated $C$, we feed the output taken from across the capacitor into a non-inverting op-amp with unity gain, then repeat. Note that I've used an AC voltage input with magnitude 1.\n",
    "\n",
    "There's something slightly different at the end, however. The reason for this is because the energy of the signal decays after each successive individual circuit. We can verify this by looking at the transient analysis at some of the outputs (Figure 5)."
   ]
  },
