    "\n",
    "Hooray! We've roughly matched the voltage levels. The initial peak is a bit annoying, but as you'll notice, the time scale is in the milliseconds - it'll register only very briefly.\n",
    "\n",
    "What happens if too large a gain is used? You start getting a saturated output, and you'll know it when the output curve gets flattened at the peaks and troughs. It heavily degrades the audio quality, so be careful to perform a transient analysis. Saturation occurs when the output voltage starts to reach or eclipses the supply voltage to the op-amp. In this simulation, each op-amp is supplied with $\\pm15V$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Unfortunately, I do not have the necessary equipment to use this filter on the song. Instead, we can compare the Bode plots of the circuit and the digital filter. The former can be obtained through MicroCap12 itself, the latter with Python.\n",
    "\n",
    "In Python, the function `dbode()` can be used to plot the Bode diagram of a discrete-time Linear Time Invariant (LTI) system.\n",
    "\n",
    "In MicroCap, the Bode diagram can be plotted by navigating to Analysis -> AC. In case you're unsure on how to continue, the first step is to give the frequency range. Something like the human range of hearing (20Hz to 20kHz) is fine. I went with 10Hz to 15kHz. The next step is to provide the X expressions. I prefer when the $x$-axis is in radians per second, so the X expression boxes will have `2*PI*F`. It's also nice because that's how SciPy returns the frequencies in a Bode calculation. The gain plot should have `dB(V(V_filt))` and the phase plot should have `ph(V(V_filt))` as their Y expressions respectively. MicroCap will do the rest. You can save the curves by double-clicking and navigating to the Save Curves tab. This is a tutorial so I've already done this; I've just put this explanation here should you ever need to get a Bode plot of a circuit later down the line :)"
   ]
