  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ae5c4f12-30c6-4095-a58a-976907c753b1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The song is free from the awful noise! Unfortunately, it sounds dampened, its lost its crispness. This can be attributed to the attenuation of the higher frequencies. Can we mitigate this problem without having to resort to higher orders? Perhaps a Butterworth filter can help!\n",
    "\n",
    "## Butterworth Filter\n",
    "The main advantage of a Butterworth filter is its maximally flat passband and a (relatively) steep roll-off. A \"maximally flat\" passband means that there is absolutely no ripple in the passband. A \"ripple\" is the variation of gain. A \"variation\" is...\n",
    "### Digital Implementation\n",
    "Luckily, SciPy has a function to implement a Butterworth filter in just one simple line (as opposed to our lengthier code for the cascaded design). Unfortunately, we still need to code in some formulae to help us choose the order and cutoff frequency. I studied this in Year 3, yet this is supposed to be a Year 1 tutorial. Make that make sense.\n",
    "\n",
    "The minimum order of a Butterworth lowpass filter can be expressed as:\n",
    "$$\\left\\lceil\\frac{\\log\\left( \\frac{10^{- \\frac{G_{s}}{10}} - 1}{10^{- \\frac{G_{p}}{10}} - 1} \\right)}{2\\log\\left( \\frac{\\omega_{s}}{\\omega_{p}} \\right)}\\right\\rceil$$\n",
    "The $\\lceil\\cdot\\rceil$ is the ceiling function (it just rounds a float up to the nearest integer). This is so that the order over-satisfies the requirements rather than under.\n",
    "Regarding the variables in the equation itself, we have:\n",
    "* $G_{p}$ and $\\omega_{p}$ are the passband gain and frequency respectively, in dB and rad/s,\n",
    "* $G_{s}$ and $\\omega_{s}$ are the stopband gain and frequency respectively, in dB and rad/s,\n",
    "\n",
    "The cutoff frequency $\\omega_{c}$ can be calculated in either of two ways: to ensure the passband specification is met exactly or the stopband specification is met exactly. You can also experiment in the range between these two values for a mix. I just stuck with meeting the stopband requirement exactly as my main priority is removing the noise.\n",
    "\n",
    "These parameters need slight tuning to reduce the order as needed. My initial parameter values resulted in an order of around 50! The gap between $\\omega_{s}$ and $\\omega_{p}$ heavily influences the order (a larger gap means reduced order). The attenuation at the end of the passband can also be increased to help reduce the order. In the end I obtained the values below:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 7,
   "id": "16651e57-52dd-4e14-88d4-d0dfbe75d1b0",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 700x600 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "ws = 1.1*noisyFreq\n",
    "wp = 2E03\n",
    "Gs = Afilt\n",
    "Gp = -10\n",
    "\n",
    "butterworthOrder = np.ceil((np.log10((10**(-Gs/10)-1)/(10**(-Gp/10)-1)))/(2*np.log10(ws/wp)))\n",
    "wcButter = ws/((10**(-Gs/10)-1)**(1/(2*butterworthOrder)))\n",
    "\n",
    "# butterworthFilt = sc.signal.butter(N=butterworthOrder, Wn=wcButter, btype=\"lowpass\", output=\"sos\", fs=fs)\n",
    "bb, aa = sc.signal.butter(N=butterworthOrder, Wn=wcButter, btype=\"lowpass\", output=\"ba\", fs=fs)\n",
    "filteredAudio = sc.signal.lfilter(bb, aa, song)\n",
    "filteredAudio /= np.max(np.abs(filteredAudio))\n",
    "Yfilt = (np.abs(sc.fft.rfft(filteredAudio)))/L\n",
    "Yfilt[1:-1] *= 2\n",
    "fig = plt.figure(figsize=(7, 6))\n",
    "ax = fig.add_subplot()\n",
    "ax.plot(freq, Yfilt)\n",
    "ax.set_title(f'Single-Sided FFT (Butterworth Design, n = {butterworthOrder:g})')\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Frequency (Hz)')\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Amplitude')\n",
    "caption = r\"$\\it{Figure~8}$: A single-sided FFT of the filtered audio data.\"\n",
    "plt.figtext(0.5, 0.001, caption, wrap=True, horizontalalignment='center', fontsize=10);\n",
    "\n",
    "newNoiseRMS = np.sum(Yfilt[noiseBin:]**2)\n",
    "newSignalRMS = np.sum(Yfilt[:signalBin]**2)\n",
    "newSNR = 10*np.log10(newSignalRMS/newNoiseRMS)\n",
    "ax.text(10000, 0.0175, f\"Old SNR: {SNR:.1f}dB, new SNR: {newSNR:.1f}dB\", bbox=dict(boxstyle=\"square\", facecolor=\"none\", edgecolor=\"black\"));"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "732ac514-d948-4a7f-88a5-67100179b00e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "At the expense of a slightly lower SNR and an order of 10 instead of 5, we can preserve a lot of the higher frequency components of the song.\n",
    "### Analogue Implementation\n",
    "For a cutoff frequency $\\omega_{c}=1$ rad/s, a formula exists to calculate the normalised polynomial in factorised form to be used as the denominator (what a mouthful). The numerator is just 1.\n",
    "$$B_{n}(s) = \\prod_{k = 1}^{\\frac{n}{2}}\\left\\lbrack s^{2} - 2s\\cos{\\left( \\frac{2k + n - 1}{2n}\\pi \\right) + 1} \\right\\rbrack,~n=even$$\n",
    "$$B_{n}(s) = (s + 1)\\prod_{k = 1}^{\\frac{n - 1}{2}}\\left\\lbrack s^{2} - 2s\\cos{\\left( \\frac{2k + n - 1}{2n}\\pi \\right) + 1} \\right\\rbrack,~n=odd$$\n",
    "Looks complicated! However, the big scary-looking operator can just be implemented as a `for loop`, and everything inside is just a regular formula. As you can see from the formulae, all Butterworth polynomials are made up from second degree factors (except for $n=1$). Odd orders have an extra $(s-1)$ term tacked on, which is a first degree factor.\n",
    "\n",
    "The transfer function can be represented as a product of all the individual Butterworth polynomials. That means we need to form circuits that match each of these individual polynomials and join them together. Let's take a look at an example: a Butterworth filter with order 3 is the factorised polynomial:\n",
    "$$\\frac{1}{(s + 1)\\left( s^{2} + s + 1 \\right)}$$\n",
    "Now, represented as a product:\n",
    "$$\\frac{1}{s + 1}\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\left( s^{2} + s + 1 \\right)}$$\n",
    "So, we would need a circuit that gives the first product and a circuit that gives the second product. \n",
    "\n",
    "The second order factors can be configured using different topologies. The Sallen-Key topology is the one we'll be using (Figure 9). The first order factors can simply be configured as an RC circuit.\n",
    "<figure style=\"margin-left: 0; margin-right: auto;\">\n",
    "  <img src=\"SallenKey.png\" style=\"width:50%; display:block; margin-left:0;\">\n",
    "  <figcaption style=\"margin-left:0;\">\n",
    "    <em>Figure 9.</em> The Sallen-Key topology. Apologies to any dark mode users.\n",
    "  </figcaption>\n",
    "</figure>\n",
    "\n",
    "The transfer function of a Sallen-Key circuit is:\n",
    "$$H(s) = \\frac{1}{C_{1}C_{2}R_{1}R_{2}s^{2} + C_{2}\\left( R_{1} + R_{2} \\right)s + 1}$$\n",
    "For our second degree factors, we can equate coefficients. I let $R_{1}=R_{2}=10k\\ohm$ which simplifies the calculations.\n",
    "$$R^{2} C_{1} C_{2} = 1$$\n",
    "$$2R C_{2} = \\alpha$$\n",
    "The coefficient of the $s^2$ term will always be 1. $\\alpha$ is the coefficient of the $s$ term. Now that we have simultaneous equations, we can solve for $C_{1}$ and $C_{2}$.\n",
    "$$C_{2} = \\frac{\\alpha}{2R}$$\n",
    "$$C_{1} = \\frac{1}{C_{2} R^{2}}$$\n",
    "These resistor and capacitor values will all work - but for $\\omega_{c}=1$rad/s! There's a simple fix though. We can just divide all capacitors by the desired cutoff frequency (frequency shifting). All resistors stay the same."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 16,
   "id": "bb13f634-a862-4b4c-a91e-c48aa7e95b0f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "     C1 (F)       C2 (F)\n",
      "-----------  -----------\n",
      "5.41675e-08  1.32557e-09\n",
      "1.86648e-08  3.84696e-09\n",
      "1.19836e-08  5.99178e-09\n",
      "9.51021e-09  7.55008e-09\n",
      "8.57928e-09  8.36933e-09\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "def normButterCoeffs(n):\n",
    "    isEven = (n % 2) == 0\n",
    "    if isEven:\n",
    "        coeffs = np.ones((n//2, 3))\n",
    "        for k in range(1, (n//2)+1):\n",
    "            coeffs[k-1, 1] = -2*np.cos((2*k+n-1)*np.pi/(2*n))\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        coeffs = np.ones(((n-1)//2, 3))\n",
    "        for k in range(1, ((n-1)//2)+1):\n",
    "            coeffs[k-1, 1] = -2*np.cos((2*k+n-1)*np.pi/(2*n))\n",
    "        coeffs = np.vstack((coeffs, np.array([0, 1, 1])))\n",
    "    return coeffs\n",
    "\n",
    "butterCoeffs = normButterCoeffs(butterworthOrder.astype(int))\n",
    "RSallen = 10E03\n",
    "\n",
    "scaleFactor = wcButter * 2 * np.pi\n",
    "butterCaps = np.zeros((len(butterCoeffs[:,0]), 2))\n",
    "for i in range(len(butterCoeffs[:,0])):\n",
    "    butterCaps[i, 1] = butterCoeffs[i, 1]/(2*RSallen) # C2\n",
    "    butterCaps[i, 0] = 1/(butterCaps[i, 1]*RSallen**2) # C1\n",
    "if (len(butterCaps) % 2) == 0:\n",
    "    butterCaps = butterCaps[:-1, :]\n",
    "    finalButterCap = 1/100E03 # don't want to use too small a resistor value here\n",
    "    finalButterCap /= scaleFactor\n",
    "    \n",
    "butterCaps /= scaleFactor\n",
    "print(tabulate(butterCaps, headers=[\"C1 (F)\", \"C2 (F)\"]))"
   ]
